#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268883 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Given that the point $P(4,-2,5)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y\\sqrt{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(4,-2,5)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(4,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)} & =(-2)\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =-2(2) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(4,-2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)}(x-4) \\\\ z-5 & =-4(x-4) \\\\ z & =-4x+21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(4,-2,5)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=-4x+21\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268884 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2xy+2$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)(2)+2 \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =6(x-1) \\\\ z & =6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=6x-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268885 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=e.$ Given that the point $P(e,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(e,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =1(y-2) \\\\ z & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(e,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=e \\\\ z &=y+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268886 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{xy}{\\pi }$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(2)}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-\\pi /2,2)}(x-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\ z-2\\pi  & =-2(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ z & =-2x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2,2\\pi )$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2 \\\\ z &=-2x+\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268887 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z-0 & =4(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z & =4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268888 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-\\frac{2{x}^{2}+y}{{y}^{3}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(\\sqrt{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},2) & =-\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4+2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{3}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268891 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={e}^{y-1}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,1) & ={e}^{1-1}+2(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1-1) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,1) & ={f}_{x}(2,1)(x-2) \\\\ z-4 & =5(x-2) \\\\ z & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,1,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=5x-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=5x-6$ (or $5x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨5,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨5,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-5⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-5⟩=⟨1,0,5⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268892 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}y+2x-y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{y}(-2,2)(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=2y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-1$ (or $2y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268893 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,7)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =0+1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-7 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,7)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=x+7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+7$ (or $x-z+7=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268894 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x{e}^{xy}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,0) & ={f}_{y}(1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-1$ (or $y+z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268965 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{a}=⟨1,-2,4⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{b}=⟨11,4,-1⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{a}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{b}=$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{a}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 11+(-2)\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =11+(-8)+(-4) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269008 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ n\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{q}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}$, find the value of $n.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}$ ($\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 4+n\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =28-4n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 28-4n & =0 \\\\ 28 & =4n \\\\ n & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269184 :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+2t$ meters per second.\nFind an expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+2t) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269185 :question/problem "A body of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}$ meters per second.\nFind an expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =6\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-2t) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269186 :question/problem "A body of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=4\\sqrt{t}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{5}{t-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=4\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4}}-\\frac{5}{4-3} \\\\  & =1-5 \\\\  & =-4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F|=|-32|=32\\,\\text{N}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269187 :question/problem "A body of mass $1.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={t}^{4}-{t}^{3}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot (12{t}^{2}-6t) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269188 :question/problem "A body of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (4{e}^{2t}-6t) \\\\  & =12{e}^{2t}-18t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269189 :question/problem "A body of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=t-4\\sqrt{t}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4\\sqrt{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4{t}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =1-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {t}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =1-2{t}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2{t}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot {t}^{-3/2} \\\\  & ={t}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{t\\sqrt{t}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{t\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269190 :question/problem "A body of mass $7\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{2}-16t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-16t+5) \\\\  & =4t-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-16 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =16 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269191 :question/problem "A body of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-24t+8$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-24t+8) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-6t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-6t-24 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t-8 & =0 \\\\ (t+2)(t-4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269192 :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=6{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}-36t+2$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}-36t+2) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-18t-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18{t}^{2}-18t-36 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t-2 & =0 \\\\ (t+1)(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269193 :question/problem "A body of mass $9\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=(t-4){e}^{-t}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-4){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}+(t-4)(-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(5-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-t){e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ 5-t & =0 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269194 :question/problem "A body of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={t}^{4}-150{t}^{2}+8$ meters.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-150{t}^{2}+8) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-300t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-300t) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-300\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{t}^{2}-300 & =0 \\\\ 12{t}^{2} & =300 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =25 \\\\ t & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269204 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}.$ Determine $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-3\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 3-5\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-9-20)\\mathbf{i}-(6-(-5))\\mathbf{j}+(8-3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-29\\mathbf{i}-11\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269253 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-(4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(4\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-2+2)\\mathbf{i}-(-4+2)\\mathbf{j}+(8-4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271969 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=8\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=6\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 8 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1-16)\\mathbf{i}-(-4-24)\\mathbf{j}+(32+12)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-15\\mathbf{i}+28\\mathbf{j}+44\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272079 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=7\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{v}=-\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 7 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot (-4))\\mathbf{i}-(7\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(7\\cdot (-4)-(3)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(9-16)\\mathbf{i}-(21-4)\\mathbf{j}+(-28+3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-7\\mathbf{i}-17\\mathbf{j}-25\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272141 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{v}=-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}.$ Determine $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(3\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{j}+(3\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0+2)\\mathbf{i}-(15-4)\\mathbf{j}+(3-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-11\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272146 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨0,1,6⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨13,0,0⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 13 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 13 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 13 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-6\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(0\\cdot 0-6\\cdot 13)\\mathbf{j}+(0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 13)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-0)\\mathbf{i}-(0-78)\\mathbf{j}+(0-13)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =78\\mathbf{j}-13\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272170 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-3,5,9⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,8,-4⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 9 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(5\\cdot (-4)-9\\cdot 8)\\mathbf{i}-((-3)\\cdot (-4)-9\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+((-3)\\cdot 8-5\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-20-72)\\mathbf{i}-(12-18)\\mathbf{j}+(-24-10)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-92\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}-34\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275151 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 5x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+5\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275152 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 6x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+C \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275167 :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}=8.$ The tangent line at the point $(1,\\,1)$ on the curve intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,\\,1).$  \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4x+12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{4x}{12y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{x}{3y}\\right)}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-1) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(0-1)+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,\\frac{4}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275192 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y={x}^{3}$ between the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,8)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={t}^{3}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+9{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =t\\cdot {t}^{3}={t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{1+9{t}^{4}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275193 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ between the points $(1,1)$ and $(2,\\frac{1}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{1}{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\frac{1}{t}⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2\\cdot {t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}=2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275194 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2xy$ along the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2t\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2t\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2({2}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275195 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ along the line $y=x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =t+t=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({3}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275196 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)$ along the line $y=3x+2,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}3t+2⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={t}^{2}+(3t+2)={t}^{2}+3t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\hspace{0.167em}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+2t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3}{2}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{23\\sqrt{10}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275197 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-y}$ along the line $y=x-4,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=t-4.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}t-4⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(t-4))}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\hspace{0.167em}t\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275230 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}}=6,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
